  "worked_solution": "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y}[x]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y}\\,dy & =[-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y}]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi }-(-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}\u20610}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{1} \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
